course: dictionary
lesson: 7616
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, so you need the context to choose the right one. Privilege is one of these words. Read the sentence around it first, then ask which meaning fits.
explain: «It was a privilege to meet her.» Here privilege means something that makes you feel honoured and lucky. Nobody is being treated unfairly. It is a happy feeling about something special.
explain: «He lost his privileges for a week after the fight.» Now privilege means a special right or reward, like staying in at break, that can be taken away. The clue is lost. Swap in your meaning and check it still makes sense.
explain: A privileged background describes an advantage some people have because of money, family or where they were born. Privilege can also be a verb, meaning to treat one person or thing as more important than others.
fact: A privilege is a special right or advantage that only some people have.
fact: Privilege can mean an honour: «It was a privilege to meet her» means you feel lucky.
fact: At school, privileges are rewards, such as golden time, being a monitor or staying in at break.
fact: Privilege is on the Years 5–6 list of harder words in England, alongside words like «necessary» and «sufficient».
fact: Privilege can also mean an advantage some people have in life because of money, family or where they were born.
exercise: «It was a privilege to meet her.» What does privilege mean here? | A punishment for bad behaviour | A broken promise | A long walk to school | *Something that makes you feel honoured and lucky
exercise: Mrs Patel says, «You lost your privileges at break today.» What is a privilege in this sentence? | A type of school shoe | *A special right or reward that can be taken away | Rain that falls at break time | A pet rabbit in the classroom
exercise: Which phrase best describes «a privileged background»? | *An advantage someone has because of money, family or where they were born | A painting with bright colours | A story with a happy ending | A place with a lot of noise
test: In «It was a privilege to meet her», what feeling does privilege show? | Boredom | Fear | *Honour and luck | Anger
test: Which of these is a school privilege? | Missing the bus | Forgetting your homework | *Being a monitor | Getting a detention
exam: Which word means an advantage some people have because of money, family or where they were born? | Patience | *Privilege | Mystery | Hardship
